# Test: `bgp update-delay` releases on CONVERGENCE, driven through the daemon
# Spec: spec-bgp-update-delay -- AC-2, user story 1 (closed 2026-09-11)
#
# VALIDATES: the release condition the feature exists for. One configured peer
#            negotiates graceful restart, so RFC 4724 Section 4.1 does NOT
#            exclude it and the hold waits for its End-of-RIB marker. The peer
#            sends that marker, the hold converges, and ze's route and its own
#            marker follow. `max-delay 60` is far longer than this test lives,
#            so a timer release cannot be mistaken for a convergence release.
#
# PREVENTS:  the defect this file was written for. `Peer.updateDelayEndOfRIB` is
#            called from exactly one production site, the inbound marker decode
#            in reactor_notify.go. That call was lost in a park-and-restore
#            cycle and NOTHING went red: seven test functions in
#            update_delay_test.go call the hook directly, at fourteen call
#            sites, so they prove the hold converges when told and say nothing
#            about whether the wire ever tells it. The sibling
#            `bgp-update-delay.ci` could not see it either,
#            because it asserts `reason=max-delay` and REJECTS
#            `reason=converged`. With the call cut, this file's peer sends its
#            marker into a hold that is not listening, `reason=converged` never
#            appears, and the two wire expectations time out.
#
# WHY ONE PEER. The expected set is the peers StartPeers had in hand, so one
# configured peer makes convergence reachable by that peer alone. The sibling
# file uses TWO for the opposite reason: to make convergence UNreachable so only
# max-delay can end the hold. Between them the two release conditions in AC-2
# and AC-3 each have a functional test that drives it through a daemon.
#
# WHY GRACEFUL RESTART. trackLocked settles a peer at once when it advertised no
# graceful-restart capability, because RFC 4724 Section 4.1 excludes such a peer
# from the wait by name. A peer without it would converge the hold the instant it
# established, which is correct and proves nothing about the marker path. This
# peer advertises the capability, so it owes a marker for ipv4/unicast and the
# hold waits for the one this file sends.
#
# ze declares the capability too, and MUST: ze-peer mirrors ze's capability set
# and refuses to state one ze does not offer. Declaring it drags in one more
# requirement, an attached process permitted to put a route on the wire:
# validatePeerProcessCaps (internal/component/bgp/config/peers.go) reads
# ProcessBinding.MayPushRoutes, so `send [ update ]` and `send [ raw ]` each
# satisfy it. The peer block below attaches bgp-rib with `send [ update ]`.
# Neither is decoration: without capability 64 on both
# sides there is no negotiated GracefulRestart for trackLocked to read, and the
# peer would be excluded from the wait by RFC 4724 Section 4.1 rather than
# waited for.
option=env:var=ze.log.bgp.reactor:value=info

# ze-peer expectations
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=60s
# The capability that puts this peer inside the wait rather than outside it.
option=open:value=graceful-restart:restart-time=120:family=ipv4/unicast

# Phase 1: this peer completes its own initial routing update. RFC 4724
# Section 2: the End-of-RIB marker is an UPDATE with no reachable NLRI and no
# withdrawn routes.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000

# Phase 2: the hold has converged, so ze advertises. The static route first,
# then the marker that closes ze's own initial routing update.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040A0000014005040000006418C0A801
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal bgp-rib {
		use bgp-rib
	}
}

bgp {
	router-id 1.2.3.4

	session {
		asn {
			local 1
		}
	}

	update-delay {
		max-delay 60
	}

	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart {
					restart-time 120
				}
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		timer { connect-retry 5; }

		# ze-peer MIRRORS ze's capability set, so ze has to offer capability 64
		# before the peer block above can state graceful-restart at all. And a
		# graceful-restart peer must attach a process permitted to put a route
		# on the wire, either `send [ update ]` or `send [ raw ]`, which is why
		# bgp-rib is attached rather than merely declared.
		attach process bgp-rib {
			receive [ update state refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}

		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				local-preference 100
				next-hop 10.0.0.1
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 192.168.1.0/24
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=60s

# The hold was armed, and for this one configured peer.
expect=stderr:pattern=update-delay armed.*expected-peers=1
# It ended because the peer finished, not because a timer fired. The reject is
# the discriminating half: max-delay is 60s and this run is shorter, so a
# release naming it would mean the convergence path answered nothing and the
# wire assertions above passed for the wrong reason.
expect=stderr:pattern=update-delay released.*reason=converged
reject=stderr:pattern=update-delay released.*reason=max-delay
reject=stderr:pattern=update-delay released.*reason=establish-wait
